+- Reveal states: content clipped to nothing by a box that cannot scroll (a collapsed accordion panel, the other slides of a carousel) passes only when a declared state of the same screen and language shows it at 1440, so designers must declare a state for each expanded panel or slide (for example `faq-open`, `slide-2`), each with its own page. Content clipped in every declared state, content partly clipped anywhere, and content shown at 1440 but cut at another declared width or at 320 fail, whatever the CSS pattern (see design-quality.md).
+- A non-default state that only script can reach (a dialog opened with `showModal`, a popover) may declare `requiresScript: true` with `trigger` (what opens it). Its colour-contrast incomplete results then resolve on the scripted rendering alone; the claim is checked against the JavaScript-off rendering (by the text the state shows, not by element position) and fails as an integrity mismatch when the state renders without script. For a valid claim, the JavaScript-off baseline is the default state's JavaScript-off rendering (same screen and language): content only the scripted state shows passes only as approved copy slots whose `placements` include this state and language (the slot's text and the headings and controls inside it); any other added content fails the content comparison. The default state cannot declare it.
+- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. A PASS `wcag-review` record whose artifact is the state's frame or screenshot (or whose note names it) is what resolves an automated colour-contrast incomplete result that the visibility backstop measured uncovered, with its faintest text (the 10th percentile of glyph clusters against the local background) at AA, in both renderings. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.
