release/skills/design-prototype/SKILL.md:13:6. **Critic:** a separate read-only reviewer scores copy and design together using [critic rubric](../../references/critic-rubric.md) and real references. The critic also compares each reused component across all screens against its shared definition (component inventory); an unexplained difference fails. Fix and rescore at most three rounds. Stop if a round gains <1 total point. A cap or stalled score is never a soft pass; send the scores and open defects to the approver. Any P0/P1 blocks handoff.
release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:13:Keep company facts, audiences, locations, names, partner restrictions, brand tokens, tools, languages and current step in project files. Fannan's instructions and memory contain only generic rules and paths. Every source has a revision or hash. Use confirmed facts only, and apply the project's wording prohibitions before copy appears in a frame. A conflict with an approved headline is reported to the writer and approver; Fannan does not quietly rewrite it.
release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:41:Use visible `[placeholder]` for a missing line, listing its slot, owner and required source. Never create a plausible headline, number or customer as temporary content. Placeholders may support an explicitly incomplete wireframe; they fail Step 9. Give the writer a text-fit request when a slot is too long; widen or reflow the layout first. Do not cut copy, ellipsize content or invent a shorter claim to fit a box.
release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:77:Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail. The shared inventory is the handoff's `components` list (one definition per component: variants, states, token bindings and behaviour); every `screens[].components` entry references it by id. It is judged structurally in wireframes and fully at handoff, and recorded as `component-consistency`; the critic compares the instances across screens.
release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:153:Six type sizes, a strict ten-percent accent threshold and a 150-percent long-text stress case are design-policy choices made for this assignment, not scientific detectors of AI authorship. The Impeccable detector's findings are advisory; successful execution is not a design-quality PASS. Card-grid, generic-hero, dashboard-trope and decoration judgments require the scoped independent critic evidence in the same gate. English/Arabic filler and common three-part headline scans provide additional screening; semantic review in every project language remains mandatory. Passing these checks removes known bad patterns; it cannot establish that a human would endorse the result. The independent critic and approver remain essential.
release/references/design-quality.md:23:Clipping is one rule based on states and widths, whatever the CSS pattern: (1) any text or control shown at 1440 in a state must be fully shown, or reachable by scrolling, at every declared width, 320 included, in that state, or it fails (a table whose columns an `overflow:hidden` wrapper cuts at 320, nav links cut by a nowrap bar); (2) content clipped to nothing in a state at 1440 (a collapsed accordion panel, the other slides of a carousel) counts as not displayed only if another declared state of the same screen and language shows it at 1440 (the accordion's expanded state, each slide's state), and that state is then graded normally; (3) content clipped in every declared state (a fixed-height card whose last paragraph lies below the edge), or partly clipped anywhere (a partly cut accordion), fails. Designers must declare reveal states: every expanded panel, slide or other content hidden by a box that cannot scroll needs a declared state, with its own page, that shows it, or the handoff is blocked. In the wireframe phase, where only default states are rendered, an unresolved reveal is DEFERRED. `evidence.json` records each clip with its `rule`, the cut elements (`whole`, `partial`) and the state that reveals them (`revealedBy`).
release/references/design-quality.md:36:Every row is BLOCK unless explicitly marked WARN. `wireframe` evaluates the structure/copy column; visual-only criteria are recorded as deferred, never called PASS. `handoff` requires both columns and all fourteen manual IDs listed below (`generic-hero` on marketing and landing screens only).
release/references/design-quality.md:46:| Generic hero | Zero vague transferable headlines, including a vague headline above three icon cards, on `marketing` and `landing` screens | `generic-hero`: writer/critic swap test on promotional hero copy and claims of distinction, with the actual hero screenshot; a headline still valid for unrelated firms fails. Not required on `functional` screens, which use clear task headings | Both |
release/references/design-quality.md:67:`manualChecks` includes `point-of-view`, `brief-trace`, `generic-hero` (marketing and landing screens only), `decoration-job`, `card-grid`, `dashboard-tropes`, `rule-of-three`, `reference-work`, `craft`, `wcag-review`, `accent-area`, `palette`, `rules-copy`, `language-overflow`, `discovery-evidence` (the Step 1 discovery ledger), `navigation-findability` (Step 3 navigation model and its Step 5 findability test), `interaction-states` (reachable states beyond the seven), `form-behavior` (forms and consequential actions, or a justified N/A in the note), `task-test` (Step 5 neutral task testing and retests), `feedback-log` (the approver's feedback log), `emphasis-order` (the emphasis order and blurred-screenshot check), `type-measure` (the type scale with measure, line height and the SC 1.4.12 text-spacing check), `colour-roles` (the colour roles and token-level contrast matrix) `grid-per-width` (the grid for every declared desktop width) and `component-consistency` (each reused component matches its one shared definition on every screen; judged structurally in wireframes and fully at handoff). The `craft` artifact may hold the last four; each still needs its own record. In the wireframe phase `accent-area` and `palette` are DEFERRED, and `emphasis-order`, `type-measure`, `colour-roles` and `grid-per-width` are PASS or DEFERRED; every other ID needs PASS in both phases.
release/references/design-quality.md:71:The critic asks: with the company name masked, does the headline still express a precise offering, audience task or independently sourced distinction from this brief? Record the compared alternative categories and the reason. If it could be pasted unchanged onto arbitrary company sites, fail and return the slot to the writer. Do not replace an approved headline without the existing approval process.
